            <ArticleTitle>McArdle disease: 2 case reports.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>161-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.reuma.2015.06.003</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1699-258X(15)00113-8</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A high serum level of creatine kinase (CK) is a common reason for referring to medical specialities. Myopathies are one of the causes of elevated levels of CK. McArdle disease is the most common disorder of skeletal muscle carbohydrate metabolism. The cases are presented on 2 patients who were referred to our medical consultation to study the cause of their increased CK levels: a 72 year old asymptomatic man with high levels of CK detected by chance in a routine analysis, and a 30 year old woman with very few symptoms, apart from slight muscle pain and tiredness. Electromyography was normal in both patients. There was null activity of myophosphorylase in muscle biopsy of both cases, so a diagnosis of McArdle disease was made.</AbstractText>
